(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-chronic-vision-loss-quiz"]={"title":"Chronic Vision Loss Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 15-year-old boy cannot read the board but reads a book easily. Which lens corrects him?","choices":["No lens can correct it","A concave lens of negative power","A convex lens of positive power","A toric lens","A prism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It scatters light and moves focus back. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with obesity has months of intractable headache, pulsatile tinnitus and brief greying of vision. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Cluster headache","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The demographic and the triad of headache, pulsatile tinnitus and transient visual loss are characteristic. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"By what age should every child be screened for amblyopia?","choices":["Under two years","Under ten years","At school entry only","Only if a parent is affected","Under five years"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, per the preventive task force and the paediatric academy. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man needs treatment for choroidal melanoma. Which is used most often now?","choices":["Radiation therapy","Enucleation in every case","Topical chemotherapy drops","Observation alone","Laser photocoagulation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it spares the globe where removal once did not. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has amblyopia of the left eye. What is the treatment?","choices":["Lens extraction","Anti-VEGF injection","Acetazolamide","Patch or blur the good right eye","Patch the amblyopic left eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Forcing the weaker eye to work is the point. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Four patients present with headache. Which one fits idiopathic intracranial hypertension best?","choices":["A thin man in his seventies","A child under five years old","A pregnant woman in the third trimester","A man with poorly controlled diabetes","An overweight woman of childbearing age"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. That demographic dominates the condition. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which patient most needs a yearly ophthalmologist rather than an optometrist?","choices":["A patient choosing new frames","A patient with a stye that has settled","A patient with diabetes","A student needing a new prescription","A patient wanting contact lens fitting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A medical eye problem needs the medical specialist. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl reads 20/50 with one eye and 20/30 with the other. What defines unilateral amblyopia?","choices":["Two or more lines of difference","Any difference at all","Five or more lines","Worse than 20/200 in one eye","Loss of colour vision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, on best corrected acuity. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has brown discoloration of the central lens and worse distance than near vision. Which cataract is this?","choices":["Posterior subcapsular","Polar cataract","Zonular","Nuclear","Cortical"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the myopic shift is why near vision holds up. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman reports a blur in the centre of her vision. Which condition fits?","choices":["Open-angle glaucoma","Cataract","Retinal detachment","Amblyopia","Macular degeneration, a central scotoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The macula serves central vision. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy is calm when his left eye is covered but cries when the right is covered. What does that show?","choices":["He has retinoblastoma","The left eye is amblyopic","The right eye is amblyopic","Both eyes see equally","He has a cataract on the right"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Losing the good eye is what distresses him. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient asks whether glaucoma treatment will restore lost vision. What is true?","choices":["Surgery reverses optic nerve cupping","Vision returns once pressure is normal","Only laser can reverse the loss","Damage already done cannot be reversed","Drops restore the lost field over months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, though treatment preserves what remains. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has ptosis and diplopia that vary through the day. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Amblyopia","Retinoblastoma","Myasthenia gravis","Cranial nerve three palsy","Thyroid eye disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is variable and mimics any strabismus pattern. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man asks what will fix his cataract. What is the answer?","choices":["Surgery to replace the lens","Drops that dissolve the opacity","Stronger glasses indefinitely","Laser to reshape the cornea","Anti-VEGF injections"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, deferred until it interferes with daily activities. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman has macular changes but no bleeding. Which findings define the dry form?","choices":["Neovascularisation and haemorrhage","Disc cupping","A cherry-red spot","Leukocoria","Drusen, pigment change and atrophy"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Neovascular bleeding defines the wet form instead. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Parents of a child with treated retinoblastoma ask about outcome. What is true?","choices":["Survival is poor even with treatment","It is always fatal","Treatment only preserves vision","Recurrence is expected lifelong","Over 95 percent five-year survival when treated"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, against near-certain death untreated. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl is assessed for suspected strabismus. Which test elicits fixation of the misaligned eye?","choices":["The swinging flashlight test","Fluorescein staining","The cover test","The Amsler grid","Tonometry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the corneal light reflex is displaced in larger deviations. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy with Down syndrome is screened for eye problems. Which is he more likely to have?","choices":["Conjunctival melanoma","Esotropia","Uveal melanoma","Presbyopia","Giant cell arteritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Down syndrome carries a higher prevalence of it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A young adult has painful vision loss with impaired colour vision. What must be excluded?","choices":["Retinoblastoma","Presbyopia","Multiple sclerosis","Glaucoma","Cataract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Optic neuritis is a red flag for it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with raised intracranial pressure develops horizontal diplopia. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Abducens, the sixth","Oculomotor, the third","Trochlear, the fourth","Facial, the seventh","Optic, the second"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the classic false localising sign here. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has months of blurring and glare from oncoming headlights. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Amblyopia","Cataract","Acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow blurring with glare is the pattern. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman develops choroidal neovascularisation. Which treatment is reserved for the wet form?","choices":["Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection","The AREDS2 supplement","Acetazolamide","Lens extraction","Patching the good eye"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with photodynamic therapy and laser. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A mother notices one pupil looks white in photographs of her 2-year-old. What must be excluded?","choices":["Strabismus alone","Conjunctivitis","Refractive error","Retinoblastoma","Amblyopia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Leukocoria is retinoblastoma until proven otherwise. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has a dense cataract. What is seen on ophthalmoscopy?","choices":["Flame haemorrhages","Loss of the red reflex","A relative afferent pupillary defect","Disc cupping","A cherry-red spot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a dense lens makes the pupil look grey or white. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man on long-term corticosteroids has glare, trouble reading, and symptoms that ease once dilated. Which cataract is this?","choices":["Posterior subcapsular","Nuclear cataract","Cortical","Congenital polar","Zonular cataract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It comes on faster and classically under fifty, with steroids or diabetes. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is diagnosed with idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Which drug is started promptly?","choices":["Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection","Acyclovir","Acetazolamide","Timolol drops","High-dose oral steroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside a supervised weight reduction programme. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who smokes asks about the older eye vitamin. Why was high-dose beta carotene dropped?","choices":["It stained the lens brown","It raised intraocular pressure","It caused retinal detachment","It interfered with anti-VEGF injections","It raised lung cancer risk in smokers"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, which is why AREDS2 replaced it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a raised vascular pigmented lesion on the conjunctiva. Which favours melanoma over a nevus?","choices":["It is flat","It is raised and vascular","It contains small clear cysts","It has been present since childhood","It is bilateral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A conjunctival nevus is flat and often has clear cysts. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with suspected idiopathic intracranial hypertension has a dilated exam. Which sign is expected?","choices":["A central scotoma with distortion","Leukocoria","Papilledema","A relative afferent pupillary defect","Cupping of the optic disc"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside visual field loss and a possible sixth nerve palsy. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a retinal mass suspicious for retinoblastoma. Why is biopsy avoided?","choices":["Pathology cannot identify it","Anaesthesia is unsafe at that age","It would damage the optic nerve","It risks seeding tumour cells","The tissue is too small to sample"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so diagnosis rests on examination and imaging. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
